ARM: dts: qcom: apq8064-asus-nexus7-flo: keep LVS4 and L25 on as the vendor kernel does

Risk: HIGH RISK by policy (always-on rails; small standby current cost).
Speculative as a sensor fix: no vendor consumer names LVS4.

board-flo-regulator.c, apq8064_rpm_regulator_init_data[]:
  RPM_VS(LVS4, 1, 1, 0, "8921_s4")                      (a_on = 1)
  RPM_LDO(L25, 1, 1, 0, 1250000, 1250000, "8921_s1", ...) (a_on = 1)
This board file defines both without regulator-always-on, so the regulator
core switches them off at late init when nothing claims them. LVS4 is a
1.8 V switch from S4 with no named consumer in the vendor tables, which makes
it a candidate I/O rail for the DSPS-managed sensors; L25 is the codec
1.25 V core. The voltages are unchanged.
---
 arch/arm/boot/dts/qcom/qcom-apq8064-asus-nexus7-flo.dts | 3 +++
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/arm/boot/dts/qcom/qcom-apq8064-asus-nexus7-flo.dts b/arch/arm/boot/dts/qcom/qcom-apq8064-asus-nexus7-flo.dts
index 9ed62a9..38917f2 100644
--- a/arch/arm/boot/dts/qcom/qcom-apq8064-asus-nexus7-flo.dts
+++ b/arch/arm/boot/dts/qcom/qcom-apq8064-asus-nexus7-flo.dts
@@ -436,14 +436,17 @@
 			regulator-min-microvolt = <1250000>;
 			regulator-max-microvolt = <1250000>;
 			bias-pull-down;
+			regulator-always-on;
 		};
 
 		pm8921_lvs1: lvs1 {
 			bias-pull-down;
 		};
 
+		/* Always on in the vendor kernel; no consumer is named there. */
 		pm8921_lvs4: lvs4 {
 			bias-pull-down;
+			regulator-always-on;
 		};
 
 		pm8921_lvs5: lvs5 {
